WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.460
Managing private keys is only half of SSH key management.

2
00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:07.240
The remote side matters too.

3
00:00:07.340 --> 00:00:19.820
Public keys in authorized_keys files, directory services, configuration-management systems, source-control platforms, and deployment tools determine which private keys can access which remote accounts.

4
00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:25.760
If those remote authorizations are unmanaged, protecting the private key alone is not enough.

5
00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:28.920
Remote account scope should match the automation job.

6
00:00:29.020 --> 00:00:34.460
A backup key should not grant general administrative access if read-only backup access is enough.

7
00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:40.480
A deployment key should not automatically have access to unrelated accounts or environments.

8
00:00:40.580 --> 00:00:44.020
A monitoring key should not be able to make production changes.

9
00:00:44.120 --> 00:00:50.200
The account, allowed actions, and authorized keys should all align with the intended workflow.

10
00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:57.680
Where appropriate, SSH restrictions and controlled account design can reduce what a key can do after authentication.

11
00:00:57.780 --> 00:01:07.220
This can include limiting the account's permissions, using dedicated accounts, narrowing platform access, and applying approved restrictions that fit the environment.

12
00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:14.920
These controls should be managed defensively and reviewed; they are not a substitute for key ownership, but they can reduce blast radius.

13
00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:22.000
Teams should know who owns each authorized key, why it exists, where it is installed, and when it should be removed.

14
00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:27.060
Stale authorized_keys entries are a common source of forgotten access.

15
00:01:27.160 --> 00:01:34.920
Periodic review should compare installed keys against inventory, active workflows, user and service ownership, and environment scope.

16
00:01:35.020 --> 00:01:40.140
A key that no longer has a purpose should be removed rather than left as background access.
